$DATUM 4-[3[2-(2-Isopropylbenzimidazol-1-yl)-9-methyl-6-morpholino-purin-8-yl]oxyazetidin-1-yl]thiane
1,1-dioxide, 3. To a solution of tert-butyl
3[2-(2-isopropylbenzimidazol-1-yl)-9-methyl-6-morpholino-purin-8-yl]oxyazetidine-1-carboxylate
17 (66.3 g, 121 mmol) in tetrahydrofuran (663 mL) was added 2.5 N
hydrochloric acid in ethanol (150 mL, 363 mmol), and the solution was
heated to reflux for 24 h at which point it was judged complete by HPLC
when the LCAP of 17 at 254 nm was ≤ The solution was concentrated under
vacuum to an oil, and then dichloromethane (926 mL) was added. The
solution was cooled to 10 °C, and diisopropylethylamine (134 mL, 764
mmol) was added portionwise to maintain the internal temperature below 20
°C. Next, 1,1-dioxothian-4-one (18) (62.0 g, 418 mmol) was added followed
by sodium triacetoxyborohydride (91.4 g, 431 mmol); the resulting
solution was stirred at ambient temperature for 16 h at which point it
was judged complete by HPLC when the LCAP of 3 at 254 nm was ≥. After
saturated sodium bicarbonate solution (463 mL) was added, the solution
was stirred for 30 min to ensure complete quenching of the excess
borohydride. The layers were split, and the aqueous phase was extracted
with dichloromethane (250 mL); the organic layers were then combined and
dried over sodium sulfate. The dichloromethane was removed via rotary
evaporation, and then THF (250 mL) was added and the mixture heated to 40
°C to distill off an additional 200 mL of dichloromethane. Another 250 mL
of tetrahydrofuran was added, and the mixture was heated to 65 °C for 1 h
and then allowed to come to room temperature. The resulting slurry was
stirred at ambient temperature for 15 h, and then the solids were
isolated by vacuum filtration. The cake was washed with THF (100 mL) and
dried at 50 °C in a vacuum oven for 15 h to afford 86.0 g (78% yield) of
crude 3 as a paletan solid. The solid obtained was combined with an
additional 40.2 g of crude material from a previous lot, taken up in IPA
(631 mL), heated to 83 °C internal temperature; the slurry was held at
temperature for 1.5 h and then allowed to slowly come to room temperature
and held with stirring for 16 h after which the solids were isolated by
vacuum filtration and rinsed with 50 mL of IPA. Drying in a 50 °C vacuum
oven for 24 h afforded the desired product 3. White solid, yield (123.84
g, 98%). 1H NMR (400 MHz, DMSO-d6) δ 7.84 (1H, m), 7.64 (1H, m), 7.23
(2H, m), 5.31 (1H, m), 4.13 (4H, s), 3.87 (1H, m), 3.80 (2H, m), 3.75
(4H, m), 3.55 (3H, s), 3.20 (2H, m), 3.12 (2H, m), 2.97 (2H, m), 1.96
(2H, m), 1.85 (2H, m), 1.34 (6H, d, J = 6.8 Hz). 13C NMR (100 MHz,
DMSO-d6) δ 159.1, 152.7, 151.7, 151.4, 148.29, 141.9, 134.2, 122.6,
122.3, 118.6, 113.4, 112.7, 67.8, 66.1, 59.5, 58.4, 47.2, 40.2, 27.3,
27.2, 26.2, 21.8. HRMS (ESI+) calculated for C28H37N8O4S ([M + H]+),
581.2653, found 581.2639.
